0. DEFINITIONS
"Software" refers to the source code, documentation, and any other materials
provided by the copyright holders.

"Derivative Work" refers to any work that modifies the Software or incorporates
parts of it, but does not include:
    (i)   Works that interact with the Software only through its public
          interfaces, where "public interfaces" means APIs, functions, methods,
          or modules that are:
        (a) Marked as public, exported, or otherwise explicitly exposed in the
            Software's source code according to the conventions of the
            programming language used.

        (b) Clearly intended for external use as evidenced by their naming,
            placement in public modules/packages, or inclusion in example code
            provided with the Software. The absence of formal documentation
            doesn't disqualify an interface from being considered public if it
            meets the above criteria. Works that only call, link to, or interact
            with such public interfaces through standard mechanisms (including
            but not limited to dynamic/static linking, API calls, IPC) are not
            Derivative Works.

    (ii)  Collections where the Software is aggregated with independent works
          on storage or distribution media.

    (iii) Configuration files or data that direct the Software's operation.

"Corresponding Source" means the complete source code needed to build and
install the Software, excluding standard system libraries and general-purpose
build tools.

"Appropriate Legal Notices" means copyright notices, license notices, warranty
disclaimers, and attribution statements that are required to be preserved under
this license.

